# PubSub Data Sync Guidelines

## Core Module Structure

### DataSync Module
```elixir
defmodule Phxplate.PubSub.DataSync do
  @moduledoc """
  Centralized module for managing live data synchronization across the application.
  Uses Phoenix.PubSub for real-time updates.

  ## Topics
  - users:ID or users:all
  - subscriptions:ID or subscriptions:all
  - plans:ID or plans:all
  - profiles:ID or profiles:all
  - invitations:ID or invitations:all
  - employees:ID or employees:all

  ## Events
  - {:created, %{type: :type, data: data}}
  - {:updated, %{type: :type, data: data}}
  - {:deleted, %{type: :type, id: id}}
  - {:status_changed, %{type: :type, data: data}}
  - {:error, reason}

  ## Usage Examples

      # Subscribe to updates
      DataSync.subscribe("users", user_id)
      DataSync.subscribe("invitations", company_id)
      DataSync.subscribe("employees", company_id)

      # Broadcast updates
      DataSync.broadcast("users", user_id, {:updated, %{type: :user, user: updated_user}})
      DataSync.broadcast("invitations", company_id, {:created, %{type: :invitation, invitation: new_invitation}})
  """

  alias Phoenix.PubSub
  require Logger

  @pubsub_server Phxplate.PubSub

  def subscribe(topic, id) when is_binary(topic) do
    topic_name = topic_name(topic, id)
    Logger.debug("Subscribing to topic: #{topic_name}")
    PubSub.subscribe(@pubsub_server, topic_name)
  end

  def broadcast(topic, id, message) when is_binary(topic) do
    topic_name = topic_name(topic, id)
    Logger.debug("Broadcasting to topic: #{topic_name}, message: #{inspect(message)}")
    PubSub.broadcast(@pubsub_server, topic_name, message)
  end

  def broadcast_from(socket_id, topic, id, message) when is_binary(topic) do
    topic_name = topic_name(topic, id)
    Logger.debug("Broadcasting from #{socket_id} to topic: #{topic_name}")
    PubSub.broadcast_from(@pubsub_server, socket_id, topic_name, message)
  end

  defp topic_name(topic, id), do: "#{topic}:#{id}"
end
```

## Implementation Guidelines

### LiveView Integration Pattern

1. Subscribe in mount:
```elixir
def mount(%{"id" => company_id}, _session, socket) do
  if connected?(socket) do
    # Subscribe to relevant topics
    DataSync.subscribe("employees", company_id)
    DataSync.subscribe("invitations", company_id)
    DataSync.subscribe("companies", company_id)
  end
  
  {:ok, assign(socket, initial_assigns)}
end
```

2. Handle PubSub messages:
```elixir
# Handle invitation updates
def handle_info({:created, %{type: :invitation} = data}, socket) do
  {:noreply,
   socket
   |> update(:pending_invitations, fn invitations -> [data.invitation | invitations] end)
   |> update(:activities, &refresh_activities/1)}
end

def handle_info({:deleted, %{type: :invitation, id: invitation_id}}, socket) do
  {:noreply,
   socket
   |> update(:pending_invitations, fn invitations ->
     Enum.reject(invitations, &(&1.id == invitation_id))
   end)
   |> update(:activities, &refresh_activities/1)}
end

# Handle employee updates
def handle_info({:created, %{type: :employee} = data}, socket) do
  {:noreply,
   socket
   |> update(:employees, fn employees -> [data.employee | employees] end)
   |> update(:activities, &refresh_activities/1)}
end
```

3. Broadcast changes in event handlers:
```elixir
def handle_event("send_invite", %{"email" => email}, socket) do
  case Companies.invite_to_company(socket.assigns.company, email, current_user) do
    {:ok, invitation} ->
      # Broadcast the new invitation
      DataSync.broadcast("invitations", company_id,
        {:created, %{type: :invitation, invitation: invitation}}
      )
      {:noreply, socket |> update_socket_state()}

    {:error, reason} ->
      {:noreply, socket |> handle_error(reason)}
  end
end
```

4. Clean up subscriptions:
```elixir
def terminate(_reason, socket) do
  if company = socket.assigns[:company] do
    DataSync.unsubscribe("employees", company.id)
    DataSync.unsubscribe("invitations", company.id)
    DataSync.unsubscribe("companies", company.id)
  end
  :ok
end
```

### Common Patterns

#### Real-time List Management
```elixir
# Adding items
def handle_info({:created, %{type: :type} = data}, socket) do
  {:noreply,
   socket
   |> update(:items, fn items -> [data.item | items] end)
   |> maybe_update_related_data()}
end

# Updating items
def handle_info({:updated, %{type: :type} = data}, socket) do
  {:noreply,
   socket
   |> update(:items, fn items ->
     Enum.map(items, fn item ->
       if item.id == data.item.id, do: data.item, else: item
     end)
   end)
   |> maybe_update_related_data()}
end

# Removing items
def handle_info({:deleted, %{type: :type, id: id}}, socket) do
  {:noreply,
   socket
   |> update(:items, fn items -> Enum.reject(items, &(&1.id == id)) end)
   |> maybe_update_related_data()}
end
```

#### Event Broadcasting
```elixir
# Create event
DataSync.broadcast(topic, id, {:created, %{type: :type, item: item}})

# Update event
DataSync.broadcast(topic, id, {:updated, %{type: :type, item: updated_item}})

# Delete event
DataSync.broadcast(topic, id, {:deleted, %{type: :type, id: item_id}})
```

### Best Practices

1. Message Structure
   - Always include a :type field in the message payload
   - Use consistent message formats across the application
   - Include only necessary data in broadcasts

2. Topic Organization
   - Use hierarchical topics (e.g., "companies:123:employees")
   - Subscribe to the most specific topic needed
   - Consider using "all" suffix for global updates

3. Performance Considerations
   - Minimize broadcast payload size
   - Use targeted broadcasts when possible
   - Consider using broadcast_from to prevent echo

4. Error Handling
   - Handle missing or invalid data gracefully
   - Log broadcast failures
   - Implement reconnection strategies

5. Testing
   - Test broadcast triggers
   - Test subscription handlers
   - Mock PubSub in tests
   - Test error scenarios

### Security Guidelines

1. Access Control
   - Validate subscription permissions in mount
   - Only broadcast non-sensitive data
   - Filter broadcast data based on user roles

2. Data Sanitization
   - Sanitize data before broadcasting
   - Validate incoming messages
   - Handle malformed messages gracefully

### Debugging Tips

1. Logging
   - Enable debug logging for PubSub events
   - Log subscription lifecycle events
   - Track broadcast patterns

2. Common Issues
   - Missing subscriptions in mount
   - Incorrect topic names
   - Malformed message payloads
   - Memory leaks from unused subscriptions

3. Solutions
   - Verify connected?(socket) check
   - Double-check topic string construction
   - Implement proper cleanup in terminate
   - Monitor subscription count
